WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.625
It's really a circular pattern.

00:00:01.625 --> 00:00:04.000
You have legitimate chemicals made by

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:07.625
American chemical companies
making their way illegally

00:00:07.625 --> 00:00:12.791
into the supply chains that
lead to illegal laboratories.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.291
In Colombia they produce cocaine,

00:00:18.291 --> 00:00:20.625
in Mexico they produce heroin or meth.

00:00:23.125 --> 00:00:25.000
And these narcotics make their way back,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.125
in a circular motion, all the
way back to the United States

00:00:28.125 --> 00:00:29.750
and Europe, where they're consumed

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.500
at great profit to the cartels.

00:00:33.875 --> 00:00:36.666
The subject of American
chemical companies fueling

00:00:36.666 --> 00:00:39.791
drug production in Latin America
is a pretty sensitive one,

00:00:39.791 --> 00:00:42.041
even though it's been
around for a long time.

00:00:42.916 --> 00:00:46.000
When we were investigating
the whole package

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.125
of everything that was
happening in this space,

00:00:48.125 --> 00:00:51.791
one of the best leads
that I came up with was

00:00:51.791 --> 00:00:56.291
a small police unit in Ecuador
that we had never heard of,

00:00:56.291 --> 00:00:58.125
and that we had no idea existed,

00:00:58.125 --> 00:01:00.375
that gets funding from the U.S. government.

00:01:00.375 --> 00:01:02.625
And they were up against an extraordinary

00:01:02.625 --> 00:01:05.500
wave of chemicals being
trafficked through Ecuador.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.375
We didn't have any idea that the heart

00:01:09.375 --> 00:01:11.375
of what they were up against involved

00:01:11.375 --> 00:01:14.541
one of the biggest
deployments of border forces

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:18.250
in Latin America, and sparked
a small war along the border

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:23.041
between Ecuador and Colombia
with this full-time drug lord.

00:01:36.875 --> 00:01:41.666
On January 27th 2018, in the
early hours of the morning,

00:01:41.666 --> 00:01:45.500
there was a massive explosion
in a town called San Lorenzo.

00:01:50.625 --> 00:01:54.291
A stolen Mazda pickup truck
packed with explosives

00:01:54.291 --> 00:01:59.125
went off and it just created
a path of destruction

00:01:59.125 --> 00:02:00.791
about 1,000 feet in diameter.

00:02:02.916 --> 00:02:03.916
You have to understand,

00:02:03.916 --> 00:02:06.625
it was the first car
bombing ever in the country

00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:08.625
and it was a really shocking event.

00:02:09.875 --> 00:02:12.666
In the end there were dozens
of people who were wounded

00:02:12.666 --> 00:02:16.041
and the focus of the explosion
was a police station,

00:02:16.041 --> 00:02:18.000
the main police station in that town.

00:02:29.375 --> 00:02:32.375
The police were completely sure
of who was responsible for this,

00:02:32.375 --> 00:02:33.666
and they ended up blaming

00:02:33.666 --> 00:02:36.166
a well-known drug
trafficker in the region

00:02:36.166 --> 00:02:39.250
and his name was Walter Arizala.

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:40.375
But everybody really knew him

00:02:40.375 --> 00:02:43.416
by his nom de guerre, which is El Guacho.

00:02:46.541 --> 00:02:51.375
Once the police assigned blame
for this horrible explosion,

00:02:51.375 --> 00:02:54.416
they started to ask why?

00:02:54.416 --> 00:02:58.125
And that led them back a
few days before the bombing,

00:02:58.125 --> 00:03:00.625
when a special police unit in Ecuador

00:03:00.625 --> 00:03:06.041
had come upon a massive cache
of specialized chemicals

00:03:06.041 --> 00:03:07.916
that are used in the
production of cocaine

00:03:07.916 --> 00:03:10.166
and that chemical is
called calcium chloride.

00:03:11.875 --> 00:03:16.000
Calcium chloride is kind of a
strange chemical to think of

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.916
when you think about cocaine.

00:03:17.916 --> 00:03:20.625
It's a very commonly used chemical

00:03:20.625 --> 00:03:23.250
for many, many different,
very legitimate things.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.791
For example, melting ice on streets,

00:03:25.791 --> 00:03:30.000
keeping dust down on
places like tennis courts.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.625
But in recent years the
chemists that produce cocaine

00:03:34.625 --> 00:03:37.250
in Colombia that's
shipped all over the world

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.125
found a really good use for this

00:03:39.125 --> 00:03:41.791
particular common
chemical, calcium chloride.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.125
To make cocaine requires
many, many different chemicals

00:03:49.125 --> 00:03:53.375
and solvents that are used to extract

00:03:53.375 --> 00:03:57.125
the key ingredient cocaine
from the coca plant.

00:03:58.791 --> 00:04:01.541
These are regulated under
international drug laws,

00:04:01.541 --> 00:04:03.250
so they're hard to get.

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:06.875
Cartel chemists can use
calcium chloride to recover

00:04:06.875 --> 00:04:11.375
and recycle the heavily
regulated solvents they need.

00:04:11.375 --> 00:04:13.125
That means, the chemical gives them

00:04:13.125 --> 00:04:15.500
a backdoor for bypassing drug laws.

00:04:18.041 --> 00:04:20.375
They can use the even
more important chemicals

00:04:20.375 --> 00:04:22.916
over and over again,
without having to find more.

00:04:25.125 --> 00:04:28.041
As a result of this being
one of the key chemicals

00:04:28.041 --> 00:04:30.375
in the production chain of cocaine,

00:04:30.375 --> 00:04:34.375
police in Ecuador and Columbia
have focused intensively

00:04:34.375 --> 00:04:36.375
on seizing that chemical,

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:39.250
so that it doesn't get
to the clandestine labs

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.375
there in the jungles of Columbia.

00:04:41.375 --> 00:04:44.500
Because once you take that
out of the production chain

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.791
it makes it a lot harder
to actually produce

00:04:46.791 --> 00:04:49.125
the quantities of cocaine
that they need to produce

00:04:49.125 --> 00:04:51.916
to fulfill demand around the world.

00:04:54.541 --> 00:04:57.541
So the police in Ecuador
formed a special unit

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.500
solely dedicated to seizing
drug making chemicals.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:03.875
That's what they've been doing

00:05:03.875 --> 00:05:06.000
so, they've been quite effective.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.541
And despite these massive seizures,

00:05:08.541 --> 00:05:11.916
you still had massive volumes
of this getting through,

00:05:11.916 --> 00:05:15.250
enough for the cocaine labs to make

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:18.416
huge amounts of cocaine
over the last few years.

00:05:19.666 --> 00:05:21.875
One thing that has surprised the police

00:05:21.875 --> 00:05:23.041
for these last couple of years

00:05:23.041 --> 00:05:25.166
when they've been focusing on seizing,

00:05:25.166 --> 00:05:28.250
is they kept seeing the same brand

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:31.666
of calcium chloride
packed in the same bag,

00:05:31.666 --> 00:05:34.041
straight from the factory
of the company that made it,

00:05:34.041 --> 00:05:36.625
time and time again,
in the back of trucks.

00:05:36.625 --> 00:05:39.875
And they called it
Finnish, meaning Finland.

00:05:39.875 --> 00:05:42.125
And that's because the
stuff was made in Finland

00:05:42.125 --> 00:05:44.750
by this company called Tetra.

00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:47.625
Well, the company called
Tetra is actually a company

00:05:47.625 --> 00:05:50.375
based in the United States,
just outside Houston.

00:05:50.375 --> 00:05:52.416
And it's a major chemical company

00:05:52.416 --> 00:05:55.625
and one of the factories
they have is in Finland

00:05:55.625 --> 00:05:58.041
and that's where all this
stuff was coming from.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:02.916
Calcium chloride takes
quite an amazing route

00:06:02.916 --> 00:06:05.625
to get to the chemical labs in Colombia.

00:06:05.625 --> 00:06:08.875
It starts in Finland at
Tetra's factory there,

00:06:08.875 --> 00:06:11.250
where they actually make calcium chloride.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:12.875
One of the biggest factories in the world.

00:06:12.875 --> 00:06:15.750
Then it's shipped all the way to Peru,

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:20.250
to a single company that buys
it in massive quantities.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:23.750
And then it moves across Peru
to the border of Ecuador,

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:27.125
and is smuggled across the
border, basically in plain sight.

00:06:27.125 --> 00:06:30.250
And then it goes by truck all the way up

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:32.875
across Ecuador and into Columbia,

00:06:32.875 --> 00:06:34.250
to what is basically the biggest

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:36.125
cocaine producing region in the world.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:41.375
Tetra is not just in the chemical business

00:06:41.375 --> 00:06:44.166
it's in the oil and gas services business

00:06:44.166 --> 00:06:46.375
and one of the chemicals
that's really critical

00:06:46.375 --> 00:06:49.041
in the oil and gas business
is calcium chloride.

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:53.250
It uses it in fracking and
other fossil fuel operations

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:56.666
and then it has like this
whole other side-business

00:06:56.666 --> 00:07:01.125
of selling calcium chloride
in sacks to the food industry,

00:07:01.125 --> 00:07:06.875
to the construction industry,
to the road de-icing industry,

00:07:06.875 --> 00:07:11.625
to airports, using it
for water purification.

00:07:11.625 --> 00:07:14.166
And when the fossil
fuel business goes down

00:07:14.166 --> 00:07:18.125
they really rely on these
other industrial sales

00:07:18.125 --> 00:07:19.375
to keep their sales up.

00:07:21.375 --> 00:07:24.375
So we were trying to
figure out why its sales

00:07:24.375 --> 00:07:28.875
of calcium chloride had just
really exploded to Peru,

00:07:28.875 --> 00:07:31.625
which was the fountainhead
of all the trafficking

00:07:31.625 --> 00:07:35.125
that was happening through
Ecuador and into Columbia

00:07:35.125 --> 00:07:36.750
and it was just a single distributor.

00:07:38.416 --> 00:07:41.250
We started looking through
their financial statements,

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:43.750
conferences that they did
with investors and analysts

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:46.000
and we found just a basic description

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:48.250
from one of their vice presidents saying

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:51.041
their oil and gas industry
had suffered so much

00:07:51.041 --> 00:07:53.625
because just fell into a slump in the U.S.

00:07:53.625 --> 00:07:54.875
especially fracking,

00:07:54.875 --> 00:07:58.500
and so they were really pushing
sales in every other area

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.500
where they could sell calcium
chloride as much as possible.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.000
And that had driven, I think, a 24% boom

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.041
in their sales overall globally,

00:08:08.041 --> 00:08:12.500
from about 2013 through
2017, at the exact period

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:15.875
that we saw these sales were
really dramatically increasing

00:08:15.875 --> 00:08:18.375
to a single importer in Peru.

00:08:18.375 --> 00:08:21.375
And so the company's
position was really that

00:08:21.375 --> 00:08:23.625
because it wasn't regulated in Peru,

00:08:23.625 --> 00:08:27.375
that all of its sales to
Peru were completely legal,

00:08:27.375 --> 00:08:30.000
and that they didn't have
anything to worry about.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.291
The irony was, the U.S.
government was paying

00:08:33.291 --> 00:08:37.791
this police unit in Ecuador
to drain this elicit pipeline,

00:08:37.791 --> 00:08:40.291
while at the same time
a U.S. company was filling

00:08:40.291 --> 00:08:43.291
and refilling that
pipeline through Ecuador,

00:08:43.291 --> 00:08:44.875
through these booming sales.

00:08:44.875 --> 00:08:47.750
And the more they knocked it down,

00:08:47.750 --> 00:08:49.750
it just kept coming back up again

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:51.250
because it kept getting refilled

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:52.666
again, and again, and again.

00:08:52.666 --> 00:08:56.375
And it seemed, in the end,
like nothing could stop it

00:08:56.375 --> 00:09:00.875
if the company wasn't gonna try
to control its sales at all.

00:09:38.125 --> 00:09:42.166
Ecuador is in a very strategic
place in the cocaine trade.

00:09:42.166 --> 00:09:44.166
It's not a producer of the drug,

00:09:44.166 --> 00:09:47.291
or of the supplies that go into it,

00:09:47.291 --> 00:09:50.750
but it's strategically placed
between the biggest regions

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:54.250
for producing coca and
transporting cocaine

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:58.041
out of South America, to the
United States especially.

00:09:58.041 --> 00:10:00.541
It's really overwhelmed the
country in a lot of ways,

00:10:00.541 --> 00:10:02.625
in terms of the violence
that it's brought.

00:10:02.625 --> 00:10:06.541
Cartels fight violently
to keep their trade.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:11.500
At the center of all
this is Walter Arizala,

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.500
who's known as El Guacho.

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:15.291
He came to dominate the cocaine trade

00:10:15.291 --> 00:10:16.416
in that particular region,

00:10:16.416 --> 00:10:19.000
which is the largest coca
producing region in the world

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:21.500
and he was brought up in a very

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:23.916
desperately poor part of Ecuador.

00:10:23.916 --> 00:10:27.125
And it was also in the
heart of the civil war

00:10:27.125 --> 00:10:28.791
that was going on in Colombia

00:10:28.791 --> 00:10:31.625
under a guerrilla
movement called the FARC.

00:10:31.625 --> 00:10:36.125
FARC commanders recruited
Arizala as a fighter

00:10:36.125 --> 00:10:37.625
when he was just a teenager.

00:10:37.625 --> 00:10:41.666
He quickly became very
adept at helping the FARC

00:10:41.666 --> 00:10:45.375
manage the cocaine operation
that they had in the area,

00:10:45.375 --> 00:10:47.500
which was a key element that was used to

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:49.625
fund the war that they were fighting.

00:10:49.625 --> 00:10:52.125
He was a very good
military commander as well,

00:10:52.125 --> 00:10:54.750
and he was good at running
a cocaine operation.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:59.291
So that's how he came to
become the principal drug lord

00:10:59.291 --> 00:11:02.125
in that region, and came
to be the protagonist

00:11:02.125 --> 00:11:04.875
behind this wave of
violence that came after.

00:11:04.875 --> 00:11:07.291
Now a conflict that's
lasted more than 50 years

00:11:07.291 --> 00:11:11.625
and cost more than 200,000
lives may be closer to ending.

00:11:11.625 --> 00:11:15.000
Colombia's president and the
leader of the FARC rebel group

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:17.250
signed a ceasefire agreement today.

00:11:17.250 --> 00:11:20.500
In 2016, the FARC guerrilla group

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:22.500
basically agreed to lay down their arms

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:26.375
and disband in sort of a
peace deal with Columbia.

00:11:26.375 --> 00:11:30.250
But Arizala didn't wanna
leave the drug trade

00:11:30.250 --> 00:11:34.041
and he took 100 fighters
under his command,

00:11:34.041 --> 00:11:36.875
and basically created his own private army

00:11:36.875 --> 00:11:39.166
to protect the drug trade in that region.

00:11:39.166 --> 00:11:40.625
It basically is one of the largest

00:11:40.625 --> 00:11:43.125
cocaine production cartels in the world.

00:11:50.125 --> 00:11:52.416
He did that very
successfully for a long time,

00:11:52.416 --> 00:11:54.500
until the police in Ecuador and Colombia

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:57.125
started really cracking
down on his operation.

00:11:57.125 --> 00:11:59.750
By the end of 2017 they'd arrested a lot

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:02.375
of his top lieutenants, including the man

00:12:02.375 --> 00:12:05.541
who basically was responsible
for acquiring the chemicals

00:12:05.541 --> 00:12:07.791
he needed for his drug labs.

00:12:07.791 --> 00:12:11.041
They were making massive
seizures of chemicals,

00:12:11.041 --> 00:12:12.791
especially calcium chloride.

00:12:13.875 --> 00:12:16.500
This drug Lord, Arizala, was determined to

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:19.750
regain control over that
region and over his cocaine

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:23.125
and he began actually
chatting via WhatsApp

00:12:23.125 --> 00:12:26.375
with the police commanders
in this town of San Lorenzo.

00:12:26.375 --> 00:12:30.250
Saying, give me my men back
that you just arrested,

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:32.500
give me my drugs, give me my chemicals,

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:34.125
or there's gonna be trouble.

00:12:34.125 --> 00:12:36.125
And so this went on for quite some time

00:12:36.125 --> 00:12:39.125
so the police were worried
something was coming,

00:12:39.125 --> 00:12:41.750
but in the end they couldn't
do anything to stop it

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:43.916
and that's when the pickup truck exploded.

00:12:43.916 --> 00:12:46.375
Which started what became
a war along the border.

00:12:52.166 --> 00:12:56.291
That pressure came to a
head towards the end of 2018,

00:12:56.291 --> 00:12:58.500
when they essentially cornered Arizala

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:02.500
and some of his men in
Colombia, in the jungle,

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:04.041
and ended up shooting him dead.

00:13:15.125 --> 00:13:16.375
They basically had gotten

00:13:16.375 --> 00:13:18.625
the most wanted man in
Columbia at that time.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.625
But the drug trade didn't shut down.

00:13:22.625 --> 00:13:25.750
Someone stepped in and took control of

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:29.250
the massive cocaine producing
empire that he lorded over.

00:13:30.125 --> 00:13:32.625
They kept cranking out the drugs,

00:13:32.625 --> 00:13:36.041
which means that their labs kept working,

00:13:36.041 --> 00:13:38.000
which means the chemicals kept coming.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:41.625
And we know this because the
police commanders in Ecuador,

00:13:41.625 --> 00:13:44.541
since he got killed, had been seizing

00:13:44.541 --> 00:13:47.166
basically regular major shipments

00:13:47.166 --> 00:13:50.500
of calcium chloride time and time again.

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:51.625
So even though this chemical

00:13:51.625 --> 00:13:54.291
which was being sold by a U.S. company,

00:13:54.291 --> 00:13:57.625
was fueling all of this trafficking,

00:13:57.625 --> 00:14:02.000
the war on the border, it seemed
like nothing could stop it

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:05.916
and it was all just kind
of this circular trade.

00:14:05.916 --> 00:14:09.000
The U.S. paying Ecuadoran
police to drain the pipeline

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:12.000
and a U.S. company refilling
it again, over and over

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:14.500
and despite the irony and the circularity

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:18.416
of what we were seeing in
South America with Tetra

00:14:18.416 --> 00:14:21.666
and its product, it
certainly wasn't the only

00:14:21.666 --> 00:14:23.625
U.S. chemical company that we found

00:14:23.625 --> 00:14:26.541
whose legal sales were being easily tapped

00:14:26.541 --> 00:14:30.166
by drug syndicates to
produce the deadliest drugs

00:14:30.166 --> 00:14:32.500
that were being sold on
the streets of America.

00:14:52.041 --> 00:14:55.166
In the 1980s, there was a
flood of cocaine production

00:14:55.166 --> 00:14:58.291
from Latin America into the United States.

00:14:58.291 --> 00:15:02.083
An epidemic of crack in New
York is now out of control.

00:15:02.083 --> 00:15:04.666
Do you have no words to
describe how bad it is?

00:15:04.666 --> 00:15:07.541
The CIA and the Drug
Enforcement Administration

00:15:07.541 --> 00:15:10.166
in the U.S. made a rather
embarrassing discovery

00:15:10.166 --> 00:15:13.333
that about 95% of the
chemicals that were used

00:15:13.333 --> 00:15:16.708
to make cocaine in
South America for the U.S.

00:15:16.708 --> 00:15:20.291
all came from American chemical companies.

00:15:20.291 --> 00:15:22.416
There's a list called the Red List.

00:15:22.416 --> 00:15:26.041
At the very top is a chemical
called acetic anhydride

00:15:26.041 --> 00:15:29.166
and it is the only
critical essential chemical

00:15:29.166 --> 00:15:31.333
that you need to make heroin

00:15:31.333 --> 00:15:33.041
and under international drug laws

00:15:33.041 --> 00:15:35.791
it's one of the most highly
controlled of these chemicals

00:15:35.791 --> 00:15:38.666
sold anywhere in the world, except Mexico.

00:15:40.291 --> 00:15:42.333
I went into this medical supply store

00:15:42.333 --> 00:15:46.041
with a Mexican colleague, and
we bought a one liter bottle

00:15:46.041 --> 00:15:48.291
of J.T. Baker acetic anhydride

00:15:48.291 --> 00:15:49.958
and the seller volunteered to us

00:15:49.958 --> 00:15:53.041
that he could easily
get us an 18 liter jug

00:15:53.041 --> 00:15:54.958
and with that one 18 liter jug

00:15:54.958 --> 00:15:57.708
that you can buy for about $300

00:15:57.708 --> 00:16:00.791
you can make about a million
dollars worth of heroin.

00:16:01.791 --> 00:16:03.916
The company was able to say

00:16:03.916 --> 00:16:08.041
that it did everything perfectly legally.

00:16:08.041 --> 00:16:10.291
Having U.S. companies involved in that,

00:16:10.291 --> 00:16:14.291
even if it's unknowing, it
just seems extraordinary to us.

